as I did. As I did, there's a caseworker. Caseworker, my brother. So he knew that that was, everyone was getting the job the same way. The same way, yeah. The scale of meatpacking in this country is staggering. Half a million people work in the industry,
就像我一样。就像我一样，有个案工作者。个案工作者，我的兄弟。所以他知道，每个人都是通过同样的方式找到工作的。同样的方式，是的。这个国家的肉类加工规模令人震惊。有五十万人从事这个行业，

10
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:51.000
producing tens of billions of pounds of meat every year and generating billions of dollars in profits. Companies like JBS are among the largest employers in rural America. But the industry doesn't just process animals. It churns through people. The work is so brutal and physically punishing that many don't last long on the job. Workers often suffer chronic injuries from the repetitive nature of the work. They can also lose fingers, even whole appendages. Research shows that there are about two amputations a week across the entire industry, which means that meatpacking companies are always searching for new bodies to run the line.
每年生产数百亿磅的肉类，并创造数十亿美元的利润。像JBS这样的公司是美国农村地区最大的雇主之一。但这个行业不仅仅是加工动物。它还在消耗人。这项工作非常残酷且对身体伤害极大，以至于许多人无法长期坚持。工人们经常因工作的重复性而遭受慢性损伤。他们还可能失去手指，甚至整个肢体。研究表明，整个行业平均每周发生约两起截肢事故，这意味着肉类加工公司一直在寻找新的劳动力来维持生产线运转。

11
00:04:51.000 --> 00:05:32.000
But in the last 20 years, companies like JBS have come to rely on a new source of labor, refugees, arriving through humanitarian programs that are supposed to offer safety and a shot at the American dream. And it's through this particular arrangement that rural Midwestern towns like Greeley have become home to thousands of people like Muhammad, men and women who fled violence and persecution, only to find themselves doing what has long been one of the most dangerous jobs in America. In 1904, the journalist Upton Sinclair went undercover working in meatpacking plants in Chicago. Then he wrote a novel based on his experiences called The Jungle.
但在过去20年里，像JBS这样的公司开始依赖一种新的劳动力来源——通过人道主义项目抵达的难民，这些项目本应提供安全和实现美国梦的机会。正是通过这种特殊的安排，像格里利这样的中西部农村城镇成为了成千上万像穆罕默德这样的人的家园，这些男人和女人逃离了暴力和迫害，却发现自己从事着美国长期以来最危险的工作之一。1904年，记者厄普顿·辛克莱潜入芝加哥的肉类加工厂工作。随后，他根据自己的经历写了一部名为《屠场》的小说。

12
00:05:32.000 --> 00:06:17.000
He described poor, newly arrived immigrants mostly from Eastern Europe who worked in dangerous, squalid conditions. These immigrants came to America chasing the promise of a better life. Instead, they found themselves teetering on the edge of poverty and hunger and facing working conditions even worse than to date. The work left their hands so mingled that their fingernails wore off, and they were exposed to deadly infections. They'd pour acid on sheep pelts and yank out the wool with their bare hands scalding their skin. With the slip of a sharp knife, many workers had severed the tendance of their thumbs. But it wasn't those brutal conditions that captured the public's outrage. As Sinclair later said, quote, I aimed at the public's heart and by accident I hit it in the stomach.
他描述了主要来自东欧的贫穷、新移民在危险、肮脏的环境中工作。这些移民怀揣着美好生活的承诺来到美国。相反，他们发现自己徘徊在贫困和饥饿的边缘，面临着比今天更糟糕的工作条件。工作使他们的双手伤痕累累，指甲脱落，并暴露在致命的感染中。他们把酸液倒在羊皮上，用赤手拔掉羊毛，烫伤了皮肤。随着锋利的刀子滑落，许多工人的拇指肌腱被切断。但引起公众愤怒的并不是这些残酷的条件。正如辛克莱后来所说，引用：“我瞄准了公众的心，却意外地击中了他们的胃。”

13
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:39.000
End quote. People cared less about exploited workers than about it. about what was ending up on their plates. Sinclair described poisoned rats ground into sausage, or how cows covered in oozing boils were butchered and made into canned meat. Those were the images people couldn't forget. Even if Sinclair's book didn't do exactly what he wanted it to,
引用结束。人们对被剥削的工人关注较少，而更多关注的是……关注他们盘子里最终会出现什么。辛克莱描述了被绞进香肠里的毒死的老鼠，或者长满脓包的牛是如何被屠宰并制成罐装肉的。这些是人们无法忘记的画面。即使辛克莱的书没有完全达到他想要的效果，

