One of the attractions of meatpacking for immigrants is that you don't have to speak English to work in a meatpacking plant, and you don't have to have any pre-existing job skills. By the 1960s, Greeley was becoming a little. leader in meatpacking. It was one of the first small towns to build a big modern packing plant, and ranchers from all around came there to harvest their cattle. Increasingly, Mexican immigrants staffed the factory. Many of them had previously worked in nearby agriculture, but these packing jobs were inside, shielded from the hot sun, and they paid better than field work.
肉类加工对移民的吸引力之一是，你在肉类加工厂工作不需要会说英语，也不需要任何预先存在的工作技能。到20世纪60年代，格里利正在成为肉类加工业的领导者。它是最早建立大型现代化包装厂的小城镇之一，来自各地的牧场主都到那里出售他们的牛。墨西哥移民越来越多地充实了工厂。他们中的许多人以前从事附近的农业工作，但这些包装工作是在室内，免受烈日暴晒，而且工资比田间工作更高。

18
00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:36.000
In the 1980s and 90s, new waves of workers arrived in Greeley, not just from Mexico, but other parts of Central America. They moved into the neighborhoods around the packing plant, which was now called Swifton Company. By the early 2000s, about one-third of Greeley's population was Latino. You could see it everywhere. The local grocery stores started selling fresh tortillas and dried chilis imported from Mexico. The schools created ESL courses. But the change also brought backlash.
在20世纪80年代和90年代，新一波工人抵达格里利，不仅来自墨西哥，还来自中美洲的其他地区。他们搬进了包装厂周围的社区，当时该厂被称为斯威夫特公司。到21世纪初，格里利约三分之一的人口是拉丁裔。你随处可见。当地杂货店开始销售从墨西哥进口的新鲜玉米饼和干辣椒。学校开设了英语作为第二语言（ESL）课程。但这种变化也带来了反弹。

19
00:09:36.000 --> 00:10:15.000
The city was starkly divided along racial lines. And meanwhile, in the background, another labor problem was brewing. It was an open secret that hundreds of workers at the plight. plant were undocumented. The meatpacking company reviewed their workers' documentation with a federal system called E-Verify, which was known to be deeply flawed. And on the morning of December 12, 2006, the situation reached a breaking point. That morning, Greeley's meatpackers started the day, like most other days. The first shift arrived at the factory before sunrise. They put on their hard hats and white smocks and took their positions along the massive conveyor belt,
这座城市在种族界线上截然分开。与此同时，在背景中，另一个劳动力问题正在酝酿。工厂里有数百名工人是无证移民，这是一个公开的秘密。肉类加工公司使用一个名为E-Verify的联邦系统审查工人的证件，该系统被认为存在严重缺陷。2006年12月12日早晨，局势达到了临界点。那天早上，格里利的肉类加工工人像往常一样开始了新的一天。第一班工人在日出前到达工厂。他们戴上安全帽，穿上白色工作服，在巨大的传送带旁就位，

20
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:49.000
ready for the first slaughter of the day. But then, just before the giant factory rumbled to life, all hell broke loose. Just after dawn, federal agents from ICE, immigration and customs enforcement, descended on plants run by Swift and Company, the nation's third largest meatpacker. It's the largest ever crackdown on illegal immigration in the workplace. 1,200 arrests now reported across six states. Shortly after 7 a.m., agents from immigration and customs enforcement charged inside. and swarm the factory floor. People ran like mad.
准备迎接当天的第一次屠宰。但就在巨大的工厂轰鸣启动之前，一切都乱套了。黎明刚过，来自移民及海关执法局（ICE）的联邦特工突袭了由全国第三大肉类加工商斯威夫特公司运营的工厂。这是有史以来针对工作场所非法移民的最大规模打击行动。据报道，六个州共有1200人被捕。上午7点刚过，移民及海关执法局的特工冲进工厂，蜂拥进入车间。人们疯狂逃窜。

21
00:10:49.000 --> 00:11:12.000
Some tried to hide in the cattle pens or inside the bathrooms or supply closets. But in the end, hundreds were rounded up and arrested. Several hundred family members gathered along the fence between the Swift Company and Highway 85. Some were crying. Some were praying. Others were yelling at the federal agents.
有些人试图躲在牛栏里，或者躲在浴室或储藏室里。但最终，数百人被围捕并逮捕。数百名家庭成员聚集在斯威夫特公司和85号公路之间的围栏旁。有些人哭了。有些人正在祈祷。其他人则对联邦特工大喊大叫。

22
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:45.000
On the scene today, there was no word. Similar scenes, played out in six different meat-packing plants across Middle America. At the same time, on the same day, ICE agents arrested close to 1,300 workers for having, quote, questionable documentation. Overnight, Swift lost about 10% of its workforce. Without enough people, some factories could not operate, and the company was hemorrhaging money. It was a crisis of epic proportions, and it raised the same old question. Where on earth would Swift find more people willing to perform this day?
今天在现场，没有消息。类似的场景在全美中部的六家不同肉类加工厂上演。在同一天、同一时间，ICE特工逮捕了近1300名工人，理由是他们持有“可疑证件”。一夜之间，斯威夫特失去了约10%的劳动力。由于人手不足，一些工厂无法运营，公司损失惨重。这是一场史诗级的危机，它提出了那个老问题：斯威夫特到底去哪里寻找更多愿意从事这种工作的人？

23
00:11:45.000 --> 00:12:27.000
dangerous work. This time Swift found its answer in an unlikely place. A federal program meant to help people fleeing war and persecution. That's when we return. Before the raids in 2006, Swift and Company was already limping along financially. For years, they'd struggled to make a profit and that only got worse when they lost a large, fraction of their workforce. Five months after the rates, it was announced the company was being sold to JBS, the Brazilian-owned meatpacker. The new owners got busy trying to solve the labor problem.
危险的工作。这一次，斯威夫特在一个意想不到的地方找到了答案。一个旨在帮助逃离战争和迫害的人的联邦项目。这就是我们回来后要讲的内容。在2006年的突袭之前，斯威夫特公司在财务上就已经步履蹒跚。多年来，他们一直在努力盈利，而当他们失去很大一部分劳动力时，情况变得更糟了。突袭发生五个月后，该公司宣布被巴西拥有的肉类加工商JBS收购。新东家开始忙于解决劳动力问题。

